 ### Native Windows build and tests
 
-With the .NET 10 SDK or Visual Studio installed:
+Install the **.NET 10 SDK**, then open PowerShell in the repository root (the folder containing `ScreenCapture.sln`). Build the Windows app and test assembly with:
+
+```powershell
+dotnet build ScreenCapture.sln -c Release -p:Platform=x64
+```
+
+The build restores dependencies automatically. The Windows executable is written to:
+
+```text
+src\ScheduledScreenshot\bin\x64\Release\net48\ScheduledScreenshot.exe
+```
+
+Launch it from PowerShell:
+
+```powershell
+.\src\ScheduledScreenshot\bin\x64\Release\net48\ScheduledScreenshot.exe
+```
+
+To distribute the portable app, copy or ZIP the **entire `net48` output folder**, including `config-editor.html`, rather than copying only the executable. The destination Windows computer needs .NET Framework 4.8 to run the app; it does not need the .NET 10 SDK.
+
+If the SDK was installed temporarily at `%TEMP%\scheduled-screenshot-dotnet-sdk` instead of added to `PATH`, use its executable directly:
+
+```powershell
+& "$env:TEMP\scheduled-screenshot-dotnet-sdk\dotnet.exe" build ScreenCapture.sln -c Release -p:Platform=x64
+```
+
+After building, run the unit tests:
 
 ```powershell
-dotnet restore ScreenCapture.sln
-dotnet build ScreenCapture.sln --configuration Release --no-restore --property:Platform=x64
 dotnet test tests/ScheduledScreenshot.Tests/ScheduledScreenshot.Tests.csproj --configuration Release --no-build --property:Platform=x64
 ```
 
